title: Pieces of a bus
mode: vhdl
chapter: Buses and numbers
check: behaves
---- intro
You can take a piece of a bus: `a(3 downto 0)` is its low four bits, and `a(7)` its top bit. And you can join pieces with `&`: `"10" & "01"` is `"1001"`.

No gates at all: slicing and joining is only wiring, moving wires to other places.
---- task
Make the byte `y` from the byte `a` with its two halves swapped: for `a = "11000101"`, `y = "01011100"`.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity swap is
  port(
    a : in  std_logic_vector(7 downto 0);
    y : out std_logic_vector(7 downto 0)
  );
end swap;

architecture rtl of swap is
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity swap is
  port(
    a : in  std_logic_vector(7 downto 0);
    y : out std_logic_vector(7 downto 0)
  );
end swap;

architecture rtl of swap is
begin
    y <= a(3 downto 0) & a(7 downto 4);
end rtl;
---- hint1
The low half of `a` is `a(3 downto 0)`, the high half `a(7 downto 4)`. Which comes first in `y`?
---- hint2
`y <= a(3 downto 0) & a(7 downto 4);`
---- solution
`y <= a(3 downto 0) & a(7 downto 4);` The left of `&` becomes the high bits.
---- done
The drawing shows the bus being split and joined, with no gates in between: rewiring costs nothing in hardware.
